Run the native firmware checks from the project root:

    test/host/run.sh

Requires GCC with C++17, AddressSanitizer, and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. The
script creates a temporary build directory and removes it on exit. No connected
keyboard or PlatformIO upload is needed.

The fixtures exercise the real keyboard, USB adapter, display controller,
transport and animation code against narrow Arduino/USB/GFX mocks.
Coverage includes physical mapping and firmware Colemak/QWERTY translation for a US-QWERTY host,
layout tap/hold and game capture, menu-only Game mode control and queued input across mode changes, encoder
backpressure and Consumer HID descriptors, nonblanking configuration refresh,
reset recovery, animation pacing, and all three mountain-bike tricks. Shooting-star checks cover burst input without replacing active flights and
launches from all four edges. Every
registered animation is also advanced through phase wrap under sanitizers.

Optionally export a mock-framebuffer bike image:

    FORESTBOARD_BIKE_PREVIEW=/tmp/forestboard-bike.pgm test/host/run.sh

The preview mock draws geometry but does not rasterize text. Host tests do not
measure panel FPS, electrical stability, USB enumeration on macOS, or Bluetooth
volume behavior. Those require the assembled keyboard and host.

Build the production and diagnostic firmware for both MCUs using the command
in ARCHITECTURE.md. Its formatting instructions apply to the test fixtures too.

support/ and src/hid/ below host/ provide the hardware stubs. host/inc/.gitkeep
preserves the include directory used to mirror STM32duino's relative internal
USB header path on a fresh checkout.

Storage tests execute the real journal against a NOR-flash mock with fixed-address
memory mapping on Linux. They cover the exact 10,000-key cadence, legacy migration,
readback before commit, partially programmed slots, corrupt records, and power
cuts before/during/after every erase and program step in either rollover direction.
They also check normal append recovery and incomplete erases. The mock cannot
replace electrical brownout testing on the physical STM32.

Settings checks cover knob and arrow navigation across pages, typed values and
bounds/overflow, edit cancellation, host-input suppression, failed saves, and
confirmed statistics resets. Persistence tests cut power before/during/after
every programmed word and sector erase during append and rollover in both
directions, for settings saves and count resets. They also verify legacy
migration, zero-count snapshots, CRC/range/version rejection, future-version
write protection, sequence exhaustion, and retention of settings at checkpoints.
Bike geometry tests check a grounded rear tire, slope contact at the front tire,
progressive pitch, lane isolation, and takeoff continuity.

Per-animation checks cover physical bike bindings in both layouts, remapping and
conflict rejection, wheelie rear-tire contact and ramp transitions, randomized
ramps without keypresses, rider speed, and both tunnel speed/density controls.
Separate tunnel fixtures test phase continuity and wrap at every speed extreme.
Screen checks cover idle sleep, input wake-up, disabled/custom timeouts, held
keys, timer rollover, and failed power-off retries.
Storage checks retain v1/v2/v3 settings when migrating to the packed v4 format and
reject duplicate/invalid bindings even with a valid CRC.
